\subsection{Creating an Index and Imputation process}
Rather than removing missing data completely, we devised the method where we created a new column in which we combined the values from the two columns respectively. If both values for the same observation were present we would take the mean. If the value of public transport is the only one present we would consider that one, and the same goes for parking spots. If both values are missing we were going to write NA. This comes from the understanding that the people who commute by car do not have experience of public transport, and would rather not answer the question regarding it. The same could be said about public transport. These two variables are complementary in that sense.
We used \texttt{mice\(\)} to generate multiple imputed datasets, and the final dataset was the first data set produced, using the \texttt{complete\(\)} function. We then confirmed that MICE worked by once again using the \texttt{vis\_miss\(\)} function.

\subsection{Cross validation}
To quantify the relationship between g27a and the explanatory variables, we applied Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression and Robust Regression (lmrob). We did this on the first dataset computed using MICE. To ensure model reliability, we performed Leave-One-Out Cross-Validation (LOO-CV), which allowed us to compare model performance based on absolute loss. OLS regression performed better according to L1loss and SQLoss, suggesting there might not have been a big influence of outliers on the model. However, when looking into predicted values, we see that robust regression was better at predicting far outliers as well as maximum values. From this, we can conclude that even though, the metrics show that the OLS model is better, the robust regression better mimics the real data
