\subsection{Variable Clustering with Principal Components}

\subsubsection{Explanation of the Algorithm}

This section discusses dimensionality reduction methods combined with robust regression modeling. The main approach we propose for clustering variables is introduced in this \href{https://www.jstatsoft.org/article/view/v050i13}{paper}. The \textbf{ClustOfVar} framework uses a hierarchical agglomerative clustering algorithm to group variables, structured as follows.

Each cluster is summarized by a synthetic variable that retains and compresses the cluster's information.\\ Given a partition \( \mathcal{P}_K = \{ C_1, \dots, C_K \} \) of \( K \) clusters, the synthetic variable \( c_k \) for cluster \( C_k \) is defined as the solution of this optimization problem:
\[
c_k = \underset{u \in \mathbb{R}^n}{\operatorname{arg\,max}} \sum_{x_j \in C_k} r^2(u, x_j),
\]
where \( r^2(u, x_j) \) is the squared Pearson’s correlation coefficient.

Cluster quality is measured by:
\[
H(C_k) = \sum_{x_j \in C_k} r^2(x_j, c_k),
\]
which can be shown to equal the first eigenvalue \( \lambda_1(C_k) \) of the standardized variables in \( C_k \).\\ If \( \lambda_1(C_k) \) is close to the number of variables, the cluster \( C_k \) is well-represented by the synthetic variable \( c_k \).

The dissimilarity between two clusters \( C_1 \) and \( C_2 \) is:
\[
d(C_1, C_2) = H(C_1) + H(C_2) - H(C_1 \cup C_2) = \lambda_1(C_1) + \lambda_1(C_2) - \lambda_1(C_1 \cup C_2).
\]
A lower \( d(C_1, C_2) \) means less information loss when merging \( C_1 \) and \( C_2 \) and representing their union with the synthetic variable \( \lambda_1(C_1 \cup C_2) \).\\ The algorithm starts with \( p \) singleton clusters, where each variable has trivially \( \lambda_1(x_j) = 1 \), and iteratively merges the closest clusters until it reaches \( K \) groups.\\ Each group is then approximated by the first (standardized) principal component.

For example, consider the cluster \(C_i\). Then, it will be represented by the first standardized principal component computed on \(\{x_1,\ldots,x_j\} \in C_i\).

