The split into 5 clusters can be further inspected in the tables (Reference to the table with correlations). One advantage of clustering variables is that we can retain some interpretability of the full model, unlike Principal Component Analysis, which has a less immediate interpretation.\\

Now, the idea is to perform a robust linear regression using the scores obtained by the algorithm, which projects the variables \(x_1, \ldots, x_j \in C_k\) into \(\mathbb{R}^1\) using \(c_k = \lambda_{C_k}^{(1)},\)
for every \(C_k\) in the partition \(P_K\). Note that this procedure is very similar to Principal Component Regression, with the difference that the new synthetic variables are \textbf{not} uncorrelated by construction.\\

