These MM estimates are consistent for each imputed dataset, except for the third imputed dataset, which shows a lower linear effect and borderline significance for the synthetic variable associated with Cluster 3, likely due to differences in the imputation of missing values.

\subsubsection{Cross Validation Evaluation}

We aim to assess the validity of the method described above by evaluating the L1 and L2 losses and comparing them with those of two other models regressed on the outcome \(g27a\).\\
- The first comparison model is a robust linear regression that includes all variables in the dataset.\\ 
-The second model is a Principal Component Robust Regression, where the regressors are the first \(K=5\) principal components. This choice was made to ensure a fair comparison with the \(K=5\) clusters of variables.\\

We opted for a Monte Carlo Cross Validation approach over all 5 imputed datasets:\\
- Choose the proportion for the training and test sets; we selected a 70/30 split.\\
- Randomly split the imputed dataset without replacement.\\
- Iterate the process for a predetermined number of times (we chose 30 iterations).\\
- For each split, estimate the model parameters on the training set for each of the three models mentioned.\\
- Calculate the prediction error on the corresponding test set and estimate the L1 and L2 losses.\\
- Repeat for each imputed dataset.\\

