The estimated average prediction errors are very similar across all imputed datasets, with only minor variations that may be due to differences in the imputations. This suggests that the two reduced dimensionality models do a good job compressing the information needed to predict \(g27a\).  \\
It is worth noting that in all 5 imputed datasets, the full model appears to be the most volatile, often exhibiting the highest error bounds when the loss is high.  \\
The two reduced-dimension models have very similar loss profiles. We prefer the models based on variable clustering and full regressors because of their greater interpretability, even though the practical differences among the three models seem insignificant.


\section{Particular features of the data}
Regarding the certain features of the data, the main objective of this article was to provide estimates of the effect of variables on overall satisfaction. The data set contained multiple variables that captured different aspects of customer satisfaction. We had to address issues such as multicollinearity and mainly missing values, so we can ensure reliable estimates of our models.\section{Conclusion for the client}
We can conclude that the cluster that represents the general food service has the biggest effect followed by the cluster of food quality, on the general satisfaction. It further shows that the other variables' effect on general satisfaction is negligible. We should also mention that our method remained consistent over multiple datasets further suggesting the consistency of the estimates.
